#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091906 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494ef4f7-873c-5d87-85e5-89094c6cc376" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "7a6fbfc8-cd6d-57fe-bcab-ab68339683bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-155" :question/problem "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $13$ | $16$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-12$ | $-7$ | $-3$ | $7$ | $15$ | $21$ |\n\nApproximate $f'(x)$ at the point $x=16$ using a backward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x) - f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=16$. We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=3$. This gives\n\n$$\nf^{′}(16) \\mid \\approx (f(16) - f(16 - 3))/(3); = (f(16) - f(13))/(3); = \\frac{21 - 15}{3}; = \\frac{6}{3}; = 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091908 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6068e54-fa91-5078-86f8-d34f1f1a3ca4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "cdf8854d-14ea-5564-81cc-4e5098f1acc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-156" :question/problem "A race car's position along a straight track, measured in meters from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ | $1.8$ | $2.0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $s(t)$ | $1.3$ | $2.7$ | $2.9$ | $3.5$ | $5.0$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ when $t=1.6$ using a backward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\ns'(t) \\approx \\frac{s(t) - s(t-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the time $t=1.6$. We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.2$. This gives\n\n$$\ns^{′}(1.6) \\mid \\approx (s(1.6) - s(1.6 - 0.2))/(0.2); = (s(1.6) - s(1.4))/(0.2); = \\frac{2.9 - 2.7}{0.2}; = \\frac{0.2}{0.2}; = 1\n$$\n\nSo at the time $t=1.6$, the velocity is approximately $1\\, \\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092031 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b175cb1-6615-540f-9145-224a838e37d0" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "6e4ab84e-f722-557e-9740-25b3a0b3d660" :question/math-academy-id "e-6104" :question/problem "The graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above.\nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=6$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=4$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/993/Source/Images/ae0cc73bdba85ba6e93ee3fd020172bd.png)" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\frac{g(x) - g(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=6$. Applying the above formula with $x=6$ and $h=4$ gives\n\n$$\ng^{′}(6) \\mid \\approx (g(6) - g(6 - 4))/(4); = (g(6) - g(2))/(4); = \\frac{6 - 4}{4}; = \\frac{2}{4}; = \\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092032 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d864064-2833-530a-b421-a57f9ec5fce5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "fb3d12e8-e413-5300-8d3f-991ddfd2a893" :question/math-academy-id "e-6105" :question/problem "A factory produces fans. The total cost of producing $x$ fans, in dollars, is given by the function $C(x)$. The graph of $C(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/993/Source/Images/5709d3ae0923e76a3e63bf4238582d36.png)\n\nApproximate the marginal cost $C'(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2$." :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nC'(x) \\approx \\dfrac{C(x) - C(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=3$. Applying the above formula with $x=3$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\nC^{′}(3) \\mid \\approx (C(3) - C(3 - 2))/(2); = (C(3) - C(1))/(2); = \\frac{30 - 20}{2}; = \\frac{10}{2}; = 5\n$$\n\nSo the marginal cost is $\\[math]5$ per fan."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b175cb1-6615-540f-9145-224a838e37d0" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "940a45ae-257e-55d1-9aed-25203f24b5f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090528} {:answer/id #uuid "9b859008-fda0-554d-8dd1-58c153af7052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090529} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f89e86-4de8-515a-aa0f-46012ac01996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090530} {:answer/id #uuid "b0efbc79-d303-51c7-870d-8ed8191d60d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090531} {:answer/id #uuid "6c53b1ff-3489-573b-915b-c3fa66c089f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0efbc79-d303-51c7-870d-8ed8191d60d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090531} :answer-field/id #uuid "54834ceb-5b32-5689-886c-6e4ca61599e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090527}] :question/id #uuid "06c09dec-29e6-5d07-b108-0ae7fffc8186" :question/math-academy-id "q-47547" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/993/Source/Images/q-47547.png)\n\n